1
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:24,720
好的，我们现在开始。那么，今天的课要讲什么？今天这节课有两个主要目标。
Okay, let's get started now. So what do we want to do in today's lecture? There are two main goals for today's lecture.

2
00:00:33,440 --> 00:01:00,000
今天这节课的第一个目标是……不好意思，后排的同学请不要再讲话了，谢谢。……是推广我所说的"奇偶算术"。
The first goal for today's lecture is... Um, excuse me — could you stop talking in the back, please? Thank you. ...is to generalize the arithmetic of what I'll call evens and odds.

3
00:01:02,359 --> 00:01:19,500
这是什么意思呢？我指的是大家很小很小的时候就会发现的一件事：只靠偶数和奇数本身，就能做一套很漂亮的计算，完全不必涉及整个整数的算术。
So what do I mean by that? Well, I mean something that you sort of discover when you're really, really young is that there's sort of a nice way of computing with evens and odds independently of, you know, the whole arithmetic of all of the integers.

4
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:34,652
我的意思是：如果用 0-bar 表示所有偶数，用 1-bar 表示所有奇数，那么它们之间有一种自然的加法运算。
So what I mean by that is, if I denote by 0-bar all the even numbers, and by 1-bar all the odd numbers, there's a sort of natural addition operation.

5
00:01:34,652 --> 00:01:49,390
如果我偶数加偶数，得到偶数；奇数加奇数，得到偶数；但如果奇数加偶数，或者偶数加奇数，得到奇数。
If I add an even to an even, then I get an even; if I add an odd to an odd, I get an even; but if I add an odd to an even, or an even to an odd, then I get something odd.

6
00:01:49,390 --> 00:02:11,494
同样地，奇数和偶数之间也有一张乘法表。如果我把偶数乘上奇数或偶数，得到的肯定是偶数；但如果把奇数乘上奇数，得到的就是奇数。
Likewise, there's a sort of multiplication table for evens and odds. If I multiply something that's even by something that's odd or even, then I certainly get something that's even; but if I multiply something odd by something odd, then I get something odd.

7
00:02:11,494 --> 00:02:29,960
所以偶数和奇数之间有这样一整套简单的算术，但你得问一个问题：数字 2 究竟有什么特别之处？答案是：2 并没有什么特别之处。所以我们今天要讲的事情之一就是：如何从 2 推广到任意的 n。
So there's this whole sort of simple arithmetic that goes on with evens and odds, but you have to ask the question: what's so special about the number two? Well, the answer is: there's nothing special about the number two. And so one of the things that we're going to talk about today is how to go from two to any n.

8
00:02:29,960 --> 00:02:54,580
我想做的第二件事，是把上次的内容落实为具体的例子——也就是上次讲的陪集与等价关系。
The second thing I'd like to do is make concrete what we did last time with, uh, cosets and equivalence relations.

9
00:02:54,580 --> 00:03:07,535
到头来你会发现，这两者本质上是同一个目标，至少在研究整数的子群时是这样。整数子群。
And it's going to turn out that these are really sort of one and the same goal, at least when it comes to studying subgroups of the integers.

10
00:03:07,535 --> 00:03:31,760
我们现在就从这里开始。这节课要引入的主要新概念——也是今天阅读材料里的内容——是模 n 同余以及它的算术。
So let's get started on that. The main concept that is going to be new in lecture, and which was on the reading from last lecture, is that of congruence mod n and its arithmetic.

11
00:03:38,560 --> 00:04:21,848
我们固定某个正整数 n，并在整数集上定义一个关系。这个关系是：a 与 b 相关，当且仅当 n 整除 a − b；或者换句话说，a − b ∈ nZ，也就是在整数的子群 nZ 中，该子群正是所有n 的倍数的集合。
So let's fix some n, a positive integer, and define on the integers a relation. So the relation is going to be: a is related to b if and only if n divides a minus b; or put another way, a minus b is in nZ, in the subgroup nZ of the integers, which is just the set of multiples of n.

12
00:04:21,848 --> 00:04:46,215
不难看出，这是一个等价关系。为什么？首先，它显然是自反的——因为 a − a = 0，而 0 当然是n 的倍数。
So, well, it's not hard to see that this is an equivalence relation. Why? Well, first of all, it's certainly reflexive — I mean that just follows from the fact that a minus a is 0, which is certainly a multiple of n.

13
00:04:46,215 --> 00:05:01,669
如果 a 与 b 相关，那么 b 当然也与 a 相关，因为这意味着什么？a − b 是 n 的倍数，那么 b − a 当然也是 n 的倍数，所以 b 与 a 相关。
If a is related to b, then certainly b is related to a, because what does this mean? Well, a minus b is a multiple of n, but then b minus a is certainly also a multiple of n, so b is related to a.

14
00:05:01,669 --> 00:05:27,327
最后，如果 a 与 b 相关且 b 与 c 相关，那么这意味着什么？这意味着 a − b 和 b − c 都在 nZ 中——换句话说，它们都是 n 的倍数——所以它们的和也是 n 的倍数。如果把 a − b 与 b − c 相加，就得到 a − c，因此 a 与c 相关。
And finally, if a is related to b and b is related to c, then, well, what do these mean? This means a minus b and b minus c are in nZ — are a multiple of n, in other words — and so is their sum, then. And if you add a minus b and b minus c, you get a minus c, so a is related to c.

15
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:50,247
所以这确实是一个等价关系。在记号上，只写这个表示关系的符号波浪号（~）往往还不够，所以我们有另一套记号：
So this is definitely an equivalence relation. Now for notational purposes, it's often not sufficient just to write this relation symbol twiddle, so we have another piece of notation,

16
00:05:50,247 --> 00:06:25,433
a ≡ b (mod n)。这是 a ~ b 的同义记号，只是明确指出了模数 n。我们的读法是：a 模 n 同余于 b。所以这只是说法上的问题。
and that is: a is congruent to b mod n. So this is a synonym for a twiddle b, with n made explicit; and the way we say this is: a is congruent to b mod n. So this is just a linguistic issue.

17
00:06:25,433 --> 00:06:53,258
那么，等价类是什么？每当你有一个等价关系，首先要问的问题之一就是：这些等价类的结构是怎样的？我指的是，这正是我们上次开始讨论的内容。我们把它们写出来。
So, well, what are the equivalence classes? Whenever you have an equivalence relation, one of the first questions you ask is: what is the structure of the equivalence classes? I mean, this is a sort of thing that we started talking about last time. Well, so let's write them down.

18
00:06:53,258 --> 00:07:15,400
给定整数 a，至少在今天我们会写 a-bar（在 a 上加一条横线）来表示 a 的等价类。
So if I have an integer a, then at least for today we're going to write a-bar — a with an overline — to denote the equivalence class of a.

19
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:38,720
那么 a-bar 长什么样？怎样把 a-bar 写成一个明确的集合？请讲。对，对——所有形如 nZ + a 的数的集合；
And, well, what does a-bar look like? What's a way of my writing a-bar down just as a sort of explicit set? Go ahead. Yes, yes — the set of all numbers nZ + a;

20
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:54,319
完全正确。a-bar 就是 a + nZ；或者像你说的，所有形如 a + kn 的数，其中 k 取遍 Z。这两种说法是一回事。
exactly. a-bar looks like a + nZ, or, as you put it, all things of the form a + kn as k ranges over Z. These are the same thing.

21
00:07:54,319 --> 00:08:16,120
上次讲的内容里这个形式看起来眼熟吗？上次我们对这种形式的东西是不是有另一个术语？陪集——没错！这就是陪集的一个例子。
Now does this look familiar from last time? Did we have another piece of terminology for things of this form? Coset — exactly! This is an example of a coset.

22
00:08:16,520 --> 00:08:54,551
用上次的语言来说：设 H 是群 G 的子群，我们有一些形如 aH 的东西，或者写成 a · H 更合适。但我们现在处理的是 Z 的子群 nZ，其群运算记作 +，所以这种乘法记号并不合适。
So in the language from last time, where we had some subgroup H of some group G, we had these things that look like aH, or perhaps better written a * H. But we're working with nZ, the subgroup of Z where the group operation is denoted +, so this sort of multiplicative notation is not really appropriate.

23
00:08:54,551 --> 00:09:13,519
我们真正处理的是 a + H，或者说 a + nZ，作为 nZ 的陪集。这些陪集就是这种形式的东西，所以这些等价类其实就是陪集。
So what we're really working with is a + H, or what is the same thing, a + nZ, as the set of cosets of nZ. The cosets are things of this form, so these equivalence classes are just cosets.

24
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:32,467
这里的一个重要主题是：当你从全体整数过渡到陪集时，会丢失很多信息，就像你从全体整数过渡到奇偶数时丢失信息一样。
So one of the important themes here is that you lose a lot of information when you pass from all of the integers to the cosets, just as you lose a lot of information when you pass from all of the integers to evens and odds.

25
00:09:32,467 --> 00:10:00,440
例如当 n = 12 时，请记住，从 12Z 陪集的角度来看，2 和 26 是不可区分的；换句话说，2-bar = 26-bar。
So for instance, if n = 12, bear in mind that from the point of view of just the cosets of 12Z, 2 and 26 are indistinguishable; or put another way, 2-bar = 26-bar.

26
00:10:00,440 --> 00:10:45,840
现在来看一个观察：我们可以非常明确地写出所有的陪集。 nZ 恰有 n 个互不相同的陪集，我们可以完全明确地写出来；换句话说就是模 n 等价类。这些都是说同一件事的冗余说法，但你都会听到，所以熟悉每种说法很有好处。
Now here's an observation: we can very explicitly write down all of the cosets. There are n distinct cosets of nZ, and we can write them down completely explicitly; or put another way, equivalence classes modulo n. So these are all sort of redundant ways of saying the same thing, but you'll hear them all, so it's good to just practice hearing them each.

27
00:10:45,840 --> 00:11:06,699
那么它们是什么？有人能列出来吗？请讲。0，对，或者简记为：0, 1, 2 直到 n − 1。
So what are they? Can somebody list them? Go ahead. 0, right, or in a shorter notation: 0, 1, 2, up to n minus 1.

28
00:11:06,699 --> 00:11:22,164
为什么是这样？有人能给出一个简短证明吗，至少证明每个陪集或等价类都具有这种形式？请讲。
Why is this? Can anybody give me a quick proof, at least that every coset or every equivalence class is of this form? Yeah, go ahead.

29
00:11:22,164 --> 00:11:48,928
完全正确，这正是利用带余除法（除法算式）。为什么？至少在很大程度上这归结于带余除法：给定 a，我可以把它写成 nq + r，
Exactly, so this is a matter of taking advantage of the division algorithm. Why? Well, at least in one part it's the division algorithm: if I have some a, then I can write it in the form nq + r,

30
00:11:48,928 --> 00:12:11,720
其中 q 和 r 都是整数，而且此外 0 <= r < n。这正是说 a-bar = r-bar，所以我们在该范围内确实有一个代表元。
where q and r are both integers, but moreover, 0 <= r < n. And then this is just saying that a-bar = r-bar, so we certainly have a representative in this range.

31
00:12:11,720 --> 00:12:42,155
此外，这些代表元全都不相同。因为如果 a-bar = b-bar，且两者都在 0 到 n − 1 的范围内，形如这样，那么它们之差的绝对值也介于 0 与 n 之间；而且根据假设，
Moreover, these are all distinct, because if a-bar is the same as b-bar, and we both have these things in the range 0 to n - 1, so things that look like this, well then the magnitude of the difference between these things is also bounded by 0 and n,

32
00:12:42,155 --> 00:13:15,800
它们属于同一个等价类，所以 b − a 和 a − b 都能被 n 整除，也就是说这个差属于 nZ。但在此范围内 nZ 中唯一的数就是 0，所以差就是 0，即 a = b。因此这些元素各自互不相同。
and moreover we know by hypothesis these are in the same equivalence class, so b minus a and a minus b are both divisible by n, so this thing is in nZ. But the only thing in this range in nZ is zero, so this thing is just zero, or what is the same, a = b. So these things are each distinct.

33
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:41,275
现在我们如何表示这个集合？根据上次的记号，你可能会想：我们要把 Z 模n 或模 nZ 的等价类记作 Z/~。因为上次我们有 S 映到等价类集合 S/~ 的记号。但这不是一个很好的记号，因为你丢掉了当前处理的 n。
Now how do we describe this thing? Well, following the notation from last time, you might think, well, we're going to notate the equivalence classes of Z modulo n, or modulo nZ, by Z/~. Because we had this notation: S goes to the set of equivalence classes S/~. This is not such a great notation, because you lose which n you're working with.

34
00:13:41,275 --> 00:14:27,914
所以通常的记号是 Z mod nZ；用于表示等价类集合的是 Z/nZ，有时简写为 Z/n，有时在 n 外加括号；这些都是同义的。
So the usual notation is Z mod nZ for sets of classes is Z/nZ, or sometimes just shortened to Z/n, where sometimes brackets are put around the n; these are all synonymous.

35
00:14:27,914 --> 00:14:50,440
那么接下来呢？按照上次等价关系的结论，存在一个从 Z 到 Z/nZ 的自然映射，将 a 映为它的等价类，且原像纤维就是等价类本身。
So what next? Well, again, following what we saw last time with equivalence relations, there is a natural map from Z to Z/nZ that takes a to its equivalence class, and the fibers are the equivalence classes themselves.

36
00:14:50,440 --> 00:15:05,199
但我们接下来要在 Z/nZ 上赋予额外的结构：我们要让它成为一个群，而这个映射实际上将成为一个同态。
But what we're about to do is put some additional structure on Z/nZ: we're going to make this a group, and this map is actually going to become a homomorphism.

37
00:15:05,199 --> 00:15:34,088
所以我们来看看它如何从 Z 继承算术运算。我们能在 Z/nZ 中做算术吗？例如，我们如何定义两个元素的和？
So let's start studying how this thing inherits an arithmetic from Z. Can we do arithmetic in Z/nZ? So how do we define, for instance, a sum of two things?

38
00:15:34,088 --> 00:15:59,063
虽然我们应该验证这是良定义的，但我们把它定义为 a + b 的等价类；同样地，我们定义乘法为 a-bar · b-bar = (ab)-bar。
Well, we're going to define it — although we should verify that this is well-defined — by the equivalence class of a plus b; and likewise we define a multiplication by a-bar * b-bar being ab-bar.

39
00:15:59,063 --> 00:16:12,440
为什么这是良定义的？这只是简单的验证，但检查它当然很重要，因为随便写写我们就有许多不同的方式来表示 a-bar。
Now why is this well-defined? Well, it's just a simple verification, but it's certainly important to check, because we do offhand have a lot of different ways of writing a-bar.

40
00:16:12,440 --> 00:16:37,400
比如在模 12 下，我们既可以把 2-bar 写成 26-bar，这就需要验证以确保：如果我们改变代表元，并不会改变右端的结果。
So for instance, mod 12, we could write 2-bar as 26-bar, and there is some verification necessary to ensure that if we change the representative, we aren't changing the outcome on the right-hand side.

41
00:16:37,400 --> 00:17:14,224
那么这是良定义的吗？答案当然是肯定的！为了完全明确起见：如果 a₁-bar = b₁-bar 且 a₂-bar = b₂-bar，那么 (a₁ + a₂)-bar = (b₁ + b₂)-bar……好吧，
So is this well-defined? The answer is of course a resounding yes! And so, just to make this completely explicit: if I have a₁-bar = b₁-bar and a₂-bar = b₂-bar, then (a₁ + a₂)-bar = (b₁ + b₂)-bar...

42
00:17:14,224 --> 00:17:45,809
我可以用几种不同方式写，总之 (a₁ + a₂)-bar = (b₁ + b₂)-bar，同样 (a₁ a₂)-bar = (b₁ b₂)-bar。这是一个非常非常容易的验证，我就让大家自己去完成了。非常简单的验证。
well, I could write this several different ways, but say (a₁ + a₂)-bar = (b₁ + b₂)-bar, and likewise (a₁ a₂)-bar = (b₁ b₂)-bar. And it's a very, very easy verification; I'll just let you do that yourself. Very easy verification.

43
00:17:45,809 --> 00:18:27,800
但这里关键的观察——第一个关键观察——是加法法则使 Z/nZ 成为一个群。所以断言是这构成一个群。为什么？为什么满足结合律？为什么这个加法运算满足结合律？
But the critical observation here, or the first critical observation here, is that this addition law makes Z/nZ into a group. So the observation is that this thing is a group. Well, why? Why is the thing associative? Why is this plus operation associative?

44
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:52,139
请讲。因为整数——没错，它直接从整数继承了结合律。这将是一个反复出现的主题；我们将会看到这个想法的推广，所有类似构造的东西都会满足结合律，因为用来构造它们的源对象本身就满足结合律。
Go ahead. Because the integers — exactly, it inherits it from the integers. This is going to be a repeated theme; we're going to look at a generalization of this idea, and always the things that look similar to this are going to be associative because the thing that we constructed them out of was associative.

45
00:18:52,245 --> 00:19:15,227
因为 (Z, +) 满足结合律，换句话说，如果我写 (a-bar + b-bar) + c-bar 或者 a-bar + (b-bar + c-bar)，这两者都等于 (a + b + c)-bar。
So associative because (Z, +) was. In other words, if I write (a-bar + b-bar) + c-bar or a-bar + (b-bar + c-bar), both of these things equal (a + b + c)-bar.

46
00:19:15,227 --> 00:19:26,629
它有单位元——是什么？0 的等价类。
The thing has an identity element — what is it? The class of 0.

47
00:19:26,629 --> 00:19:51,892
它有逆元——n 减……完全正确。有几种不同的写法。对于 a-bar 的逆元：以前我们用幂次记号，因为我们把群写成乘法形式——就好像运算是乘法一样。
It has inverses — n minus... absolutely. There are a couple of different ways of writing it. So the inverse of a-bar: previously we'd used a power notation because we'd written our groups very multiplicatively — we'd written it as if the operation was multiplication.

48
00:19:51,892 --> 00:20:18,640
而在这里我们的群是用加号写的，所以很自然把元素的逆元写成负的该元素。正如刚才有人指出的，写法有很多种，可以写成 (n − a)-bar，或者直接写成 -a-bar。
Here we're writing our group with a plus sign, so it's very natural to write as the inverse of an element minus that thing. And so as someone pointed out, this is, well, either — there are many ways of writing this, but one can write it as (n - a)-bar, or just -a-bar.

49
00:20:18,640 --> 00:20:55,616
用上次的语言来说，再次回到我们之前的结论：用上次的语言，Z 的子群 nZ 的陪集集合构成了一个群。
And so in the language from last time, again just returning to what we did before: in the language from last time, the set of cosets of the subgroup nZ of Z forms a group.

50
00:20:55,616 --> 00:21:08,063
nZ 是 Z 的正规子群吗？请讲。是的，没错！因为 Z 是交换群（阿贝尔群）。
Is nZ a normal subgroup of Z? Go ahead. Yes, yes it is! It is because Z is an abelian group.

51
00:21:08,063 --> 00:21:28,621
到头来你会发现，对群的任意子群这并不成立：并非随便取一个子群，其陪集集合就能构成一个群；但对正规子群而言这确实成立。不过在整数群这里这很平凡，因为 Z 是交换群，所以每个子群都是正规子群。
And it's going to turn out that this isn't going to be true for arbitrary subgroups of a group: it's not going to be true that if I just have any subgroup, then the set of cosets are going to form a group; but it is going to be true in the case of normal subgroups. But here that's just a trivial thing because Z is abelian, so every subgroup is a normal subgroup.

52
00:21:28,621 --> 00:21:41,796
清楚了吗？有什么问题吗？很好。请讲。您在问有没有问题之前说的那长长的一串，能重复一遍吗？
Is that clear? Are there any questions? Great. Oh, go ahead. You just the long thing that you said just before any questions, could you repeat that?

53
00:21:41,796 --> 00:22:15,760
哦，我刚才只是说，对任意子群来说这不成立——我们稍后会看到这一点，群的任意子群的陪集集合并不一定构成群；但在子群正规的假设下是成立的。在 Z 的情形下，这是一个平凡的假设，因为它的所有子群都是正规的。
Oh, I was just saying that, so it's not true that if I have some arbitrary subgroup — it's not going to be true, we're going to see this later on, but it's not going to be true that if I have an arbitrary subgroup of a group, then the set of cosets of that subgroup forms a group; but it will be true under the hypothesis that H is normal. In the case of Z, this is a trivial hypothesis, because all of its subgroups are normal.

54
00:22:17,839 --> 00:22:49,760
另一个观察是：一周前有人问："对每个 n，都存在 n 阶循环群吗？"当时我们给出了 n 个字母上的对称群 S_n 的一个子群作为例子，它是 n 阶循环群——也就是轮换 (1 2 ... n)，1 映到 2，2 映到 3 直到 n 映回 1。
So another observation: a week ago somebody asked the question, "Is there a cyclic group of order n for every n?" And we gave an example of a subgroup of S_n, the symmetric group on n letters, which was cyclic of order n — just this sort of cycle that goes 1 -> 2, 2 -> 3, up to n which goes back to 1.

55
00:22:49,760 --> 00:23:19,867
现在有另一个构造这种群的绝妙方式：Z/nZ 也是 n 阶循环群，它是n 阶循环群的一个很好的模型。记住，我们证明过所有 n 阶循环群都是同构的，所以这里提供了一种研究 n 阶循环群的极简方式。
Well, here's another nice construction of such a group: Z/nZ is also cyclic of order n, and it's a nice model for a cyclic group of order n. Remember, we saw that all cyclic groups of order n are isomorphic, so here is a very simple way of studying cyclic groups of order n.

56
00:23:19,867 --> 00:23:54,083
它当然就是由 1-bar 生成的，因为如果把 1-bar 自身相加 k 次，得到的就是k-bar。所以我能得到 1-bar, 2-bar 一直到 (n − 1)-bar，以及 n-bar，也就是 0-bar。
Of course, this is just generated by 1-bar, because if I add 1-bar to itself k times, then this thing is just k-bar. So I get all of 1-bar, 2-bar, up to (n - 1)-bar, and n-bar, which is just 0-bar.

57
00:23:54,083 --> 00:24:10,772
"不好意思，我不太确定为什么由 1……" 那么为什么……问题是：为什么由 1-bar 生成的循环群等于 Z/nZ？
"Excuse me, I hate to say, I'm not exactly sure why 1..." So why is... The question is, why is the cyclic group generated by 1-bar equal to Z/nZ?

58
00:24:10,772 --> 00:24:42,703
循环群是什么？它就是所有——在乘法记号下是各次幂，但在我们的记号中幂次并不合适，我们要的是倍数——所以这些元素形如 g + g（相加 n 次），或者在负幂时放入逆元，当 n 取遍 Z。
Well, what is the cyclic group? It's the set of — well, in multiplicative notation, powers, but in our notation powers are inappropriate, we want multiples — so these are things that look like g + g (n times), or when there are negative powers, putting in the inverses, as n ranges over Z.

59
00:24:42,703 --> 00:24:55,667
特别地，由 1-bar 生成的循环子群包含：1-bar、1-bar + 1-bar、1-bar + 1-bar + 1-bar……所以它当然包含 1-bar 自身相加 k 次。
In particular, the cyclic subgroup generated by 1-bar contains: 1-bar, 1-bar + 1-bar, 1-bar + 1-bar + 1-bar... so it certainly contains 1-bar added to itself k times.

60
00:24:55,667 --> 00:25:11,697
如果我把 1-bar 自身相加 k 次，根据结合律我就得到 k-bar：1-bar + ... + 1-bar 就是 (1 + ... + 1 共 k 项)-bar，即 k-bar。
Well, if I add 1-bar to itself k times, then I get k-bar because of associativity: 1-bar + ... + 1-bar just looks like (1 + ... + 1 k times)-bar, so that's k-bar.

61
00:25:11,697 --> 00:25:21,720
因此特别地，由 1-bar 生成的循环子群中包含：1-bar, 2-bar 一直到 (n − 1)-bar，以及 n-bar，也就是0-bar。
So in particular, I have in the cyclic subgroup generated by 1-bar: 1-bar, 2-bar, all the way up to (n - 1)-bar, and n-bar, which is 0-bar.

62
00:25:21,720 --> 00:25:46,399
但我们之前看到这已经耗尽了 Z/nZ 的所有元素。所以如果这里面的所有元素都在循环子群中，那两者必然相等。清楚了吗？很好。
But we saw before that exhausts all the elements of Z/nZ. So if everything that's in here is in here, then these must be equal. Is that clear? Great.

63
00:25:47,800 --> 00:26:26,359
我顺便把我写在那边的记号再说得更明确一点：当我们用 + 作为群运算时，我们用 n · g 表示 g 自身相加 n 次，而不是乘法记号 g^n。
So I'll maybe just make something that I wrote over there even more explicit notation: when we use + for our group operation, we write n * g for g added to itself n times, not the multiplicative notation g^n.

64
00:26:26,359 --> 00:26:47,136
所以要小心，因为我们在 Z/nZ 上定义了两种运算：它们是不同的运算，记号也不同。
So be careful, because for instance there are certainly two operations that we've defined on Z/nZ: they're different operations and they have different notations for them.

65
00:26:47,136 --> 00:27:32,229
好，关于这两个运算我们再做一个观察： Z/nZ 上的加法和乘法满足分配律，精确含义是：a-bar · (b-bar + c-bar) 等同于 a-bar · b-bar+ a-bar · c-bar。
Okay, so let's make a further observation about just these two things: here's a further observation. The addition and multiplication on Z/nZ distribute in the precise sense that if I multiply a-bar * (b-bar + c-bar), then this is the same as a-bar * b-bar + a-bar * c-bar.

66
00:27:32,229 --> 00:27:49,839
这当然显得极其显然，因为它只是从整数继承了这条性质。这直接继承自 Z，验证起来非常简单。
Now of course this seems utterly obvious, because it's just inheriting what's going on from the integers. So this is inherited from Z, and so it's a very simple verification.

67
00:27:49,839 --> 00:28:10,208
但这仍然是非常重要的一点，并将成为反复出现的主题，因为接下来我们会有许多结构具有极为相似的性质。当我们进入课程第二部分的环论时，这更将成为核心主题。
Nonetheless, it's a very important point: it becomes a repeated theme because we're going to have a lot of things that work very similarly to this, and this is going to be a major theme when we get to the second part of the course on ring theory, this will be a major theme.

68
00:28:10,208 --> 00:28:49,854
好，这里举一个展示模算术威力的例子。有人能告诉我怎样计算 2^1000 十进制展开中的最后两位数字吗？请讲。
Okay, so here's an example of the usefulness of modular arithmetic. Can somebody tell me how to compute the last two digits in the decimal expansion of 2^1000? Go ahead.

69
00:28:49,854 --> 00:29:17,812
完全正确！答案是：我们只需计算 2^1000 mod 100，这让整个问题变得极其容易处理。
Exactly! So the answer is: all we have to compute is 2^1000 mod 100, which makes the whole problem extremely manageable.

70
00:29:17,812 --> 00:29:34,000
我们完全明确地把它算出来，看看这有多简单；这也是计算机使用的一项关键技术——在实际中当你需要做类似计算 2^1000 最后几位的事情时，
Completely explicitly, just to show you how easy this is, and also an essential technique used by computers — in the case that you need to compute something analogous to the last couple digits of 2^1000,

71
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:46,319
计算机就是这样算的：它们不会硬算出 2^1000 的全部数值（那是一个非常庞大的数字）再转换成十进制，那效率太低了。
for instance, this is how computers do it: they don't crank out all of 2^1000, because that's a rather large number, and convert it to decimal notation; that would be not so efficient.

72
00:29:46,319 --> 00:30:04,807
这里有一种极其简单的速算方法：2^10 = 1024 ≡ 24 (mod 100)。我做这个计算一部分原因也是因为它很有用——大家得习惯用这种记号写算式。
So here's a very simple, short way of computing the thing: 2^10 = 1024, which is congruent to 24 mod 100. I do this in part because it's useful — you have to get used to writing things in this kind of notation.

73
00:30:04,807 --> 00:30:36,531
如果把它平方，即 2^20——也就是 (2^10)^2，这意味着它是 24^2 mod 100，等于 576 ≡ 76 (mod 100)。到目前为止的计算有疑问吗？大家都清楚了吗？
If I square this, so 2^20 — well, that's (2^10)^2, and that means this is 24^2 mod 100, which is 576, which is congruent to 76 mod 100. Are there any questions about the computation so far? Is this clear to everyone?

74
00:30:36,531 --> 00:31:03,342
现在如果把 76 平方，得到 5776 ≡ 76 (mod 100)。76^n mod 100 是多少？76。这表明通过归纳法，76^n ≡ 76 (mod 100)。
Now if I square 76, this is 5776, which is just 76 mod 100. What is 76^n mod 100? 76. This implies that 76^n is congruent to 76 mod 100 by induction.

75
00:31:03,342 --> 00:31:28,639
那么 2^20 是 76，而原式就是把 2^20 取 50 次方——换句话说，2^1000 就是 (2^20)^50 ≡ 76^50 (mod 100)。所以 2^1000 ≡ 76 (mod 100)。问题就解决了，非常简单。
And so, well, this thing is 2^20, which is, you know, the 50th power — in other words, 2^1000 is (2^20)^50, which is 76^50 mod 100. So 2^1000 is just congruent to 76 mod 100. So the question is answered: very, very simple.

76
00:31:38,880 --> 00:32:42,701
所以到目前为止模算术给了我们一个群。那么 Z/nZ 在乘法下是一个群吗？请讲。没错！这绝对不是群，不是，因为例如 0-bar 不可能有逆元，因为乘上任何东西都得到 0。
So so far modular arithmetic has given us one group. Is Z/nZ with multiplication a group? Somebody... yeah, go ahead. Right! So this is definitely not a group, no, because for instance 0-bar cannot possibly have an inverse, because anything you multiply by gives you zero.

77
00:32:42,701 --> 00:33:14,679
但有人能看出 Z/nZ 的哪个子集能构成群吗？请讲。完全正确！做最朴素的处理就行：这里的问题在于有些元素没有逆元，所以只需取出那些拥有逆元的元素。
But can somebody see a subset of Z/nZ which gives a group? Go ahead. Exactly! You just do the simplest thing: the problem here is that there are elements without inverses, so you just take the ones that have inverses.

78
00:33:16,360 --> 00:33:47,112
因此我们确实得到了 Z/nZ 的一个子集，在乘法下构成群：具体来说，就是拥有逆元的元素集合：
So we do have a subset of Z/nZ which gives a group under multiplication: explicitly, the set of things that have inverses.

79
00:33:47,112 --> 00:34:20,326
即所有满足存在 c-bar ∈ Z/nZ 使得 a-bar · c-bar = 1-bar 的 a-bar ∈ Z/nZ。这个集合的记号是 (Z/nZ)^×。
So the a-bar in Z/nZ such that there is c-bar in Z/nZ such that a-bar * c-bar = 1-bar. And the notation for this is (Z/nZ)^×.

80
00:34:20,326 --> 00:34:55,553
大家对这个记号应该很熟悉，因为讨论实数和复数时，我们加了一个右上角乘号来表示非零元素；而那些正是可逆元，所以这里的记号是完全一致的。好，为了确保这一点完全清楚：
This should be a familiar notation to you, because when we talked about the real numbers and complex numbers, we added a cross to denote the non-zero elements; well, those are exactly the elements which have inverses, so this is an entirely consistent notation.

81
00:34:55,553 --> 00:35:16,429
结合律同样继承自整数。单位元：我已经说过了，就是 1-bar。1-bar 有自然的逆元，即它自己，所以它肯定在里面。至于逆元：按定义由构造保证。
The identity: well, I've already said what it is, it's 1-bar. 1-bar has a natural inverse, namely itself, so it's certainly in there. And the inverses: that's by construction.

82
00:35:16,429 --> 00:35:57,639
现在让我们简要回顾一下最大公因数（gcd），因为我们想搞清这个群的结构——想知道它长什么样，而这恰好联系着一些非常初等的算术性质。为了理解 (Z/nZ)^×，我们复习最大公因数。
So let's have a brief recall on gcds, because we want to discover the structure of this group — you want to know what this looks like, and it turns out that this is tied up with some very, very elementary arithmetic. So to understand (Z/nZ)^×: review of greatest common divisors.

83
00:35:57,639 --> 00:36:15,951
设 m 和 n 为整数，我假设它们不全为零——这个假设只是为了定理写起来更方便——那么什么是 gcd？
So if I have integers m and n, and I'm going to assume they're not both zero — that's just a hypothesis to make theorems easier to write down — so what is the gcd?

84
00:36:15,951 --> 00:36:44,133
你在小学可能学过它是能同时整除 m 和 n 的最大正整数——其中"正"是定义中稍显多余的限定——也就是整除 m 和 n 的最大整数。
Well, you were probably taught this in elementary school as the largest positive integer — where "positive" is a redundant part of the definition — the largest integer dividing m and n.

85
00:36:44,133 --> 00:37:05,405
但为了从代数上理解 gcd，当然有一种更好的表述方式，因为它不依赖从实数继承来的整数大小顺序结构；换句话说，它不涉及正负概念。
But for the purposes of understanding the gcd, there's of course a better way of writing this down, because it doesn't make reference to the order structure on the integers inherited from the real numbers; in other words, it doesn't talk about positives and negatives.

86
00:37:05,405 --> 00:37:34,892
纯粹由乘法（或者说整除关系）就能诱导出另一种偏序结构。所以 gcd 是唯一的正整数 d，使得 d 整除 m 和 n，且若 e 整除 m 和 n，则 e 整除 d。
There's another order structure induced just by multiplication, or in other words the divisibility relation. So this is the unique positive integer d such that d divides m and n, and if e divides m and n, then e divides d.

87
00:37:34,892 --> 00:37:50,280
这非常容易验证，大家在其他课程里肯定也做过：最大公因数具有这个性质。
And it's very easy to verify, of course — you've surely done this in another course — that this number has this property.

88
00:37:53,080 --> 00:38:15,975
现在关于 gcd 我想证明的主要事实是：它与我们学过的 Z 的子群结构密切相关。
Now the main fact that I want to prove about gcds is that they have a relationship to the subgroup structure on Z that we've studied so far.

89
00:38:15,975 --> 00:38:45,960
请问有疑问吗？有问题？抱歉，什么问题？哦，唯一使得 d 整除 m, n，且任意公因数 e 都整除 d 的正整数 d。所以它只是用整除结构来表述的。
Yes? Question? Sorry, what's the question? Oh, unique positive integer d such that d divides m and n, and if e divides m and n, then e divides d. So it's just talking about the divisibility structure of the thing.

90
00:38:45,960 --> 00:39:08,988
现在看 mZ + nZ：首先，它们都是 Z 的子群，mZ + nZ 是不是 Z 的子群？答案是肯定的，这不难看出：0 显然在其中，
So mZ + nZ: first question, these are both subgroups of Z, is mZ + nZ a subgroup of Z? The answer is yes, and it's not hard to see: 0 is in here, it's closed under addition,

91
00:39:08,988 --> 00:39:22,015
它在加法下封闭，而且元素的相反数也落在其中，因为元素都形如 mr + ns，取相反数即可。所以这确实是一个子群。
and the inverse of something in here is again in here, because elements are of the form mr + ns, and just negate those. So this is certainly a subgroup.

92
00:39:22,015 --> 00:39:47,600
但我们知道 Z 的所有子群都形如某个正整数乘 Z（即 dZ）。那么猜猜这是什么？请讲。dZ——没错，答案就是 gcd(m, n)Z！这可真难猜啊！
But we know what all subgroups of Z look like: they look like dZ for some positive integer d. So any guesses as to what this is? Go ahead. dZ — exactly, the answer is gcd(m, n)Z! That was a hard one to guess!

93
00:39:47,600 --> 00:40:14,976
证明：这是一个相当容易的验证，我就快速过一遍：正如我所说，它是 Z 的子群，所以它等于 dZ，对某个正整数 d。
Proof: well, this is a fairly easy verification, but I'll go through it quickly: as I said, this is a subgroup of Z, so it equals dZ for some d, and we can assume d is a positive integer.

94
00:40:14,976 --> 00:40:43,426
既然 m 属于 mZ + nZ（也就是 dZ），d 当然整除 m；同理，d 也整除 n。所以特征性质的第一部分得到了验证。
Now since m is in mZ + nZ, which is exactly dZ, d certainly divides m; by the same argument, d divides n. So the first part of this characterizing property is verified.

95
00:40:43,426 --> 00:41:19,007
第二部分：假设 e 整除 m 和 n。因为 d ∈ dZ = mZ + nZ，我可以将 d 写成 mr + ns；若 e 整除 m 且 e 整除 n，则 e 整除 mr + ns，从而 e 整除 d。清楚了吗？
Second part: suppose e divides m and n. Because d is in dZ = mZ + nZ, I can write d as mr + ns; and if e divides m and e divides n, then e divides mr + ns, and hence e divides d. Is that clear?

96
00:41:19,007 --> 00:41:36,079
这正是 gcd 的特征性质：d 整除 m 和 n，且若公因数整除 m 和 n 则整除 d。所以我们验证了所有性质，证明了这个引理。
So this is exactly the characterizing property: d divides m and n, and if e divides m and n, then e divides d. So we verified the properties, and we've proved the lemma.

97
00:41:38,800 --> 00:42:28,080
好，现在让我们利用这个事实来证明命题： (Z/nZ)^× 恰好就是由满足条件的 a-bar ∈ Z/nZ 组成的集合——猜猜条件是什么？没错：gcd(a, n) = 1；或者用更简洁的术语说，a 与 n 互素。
go. Now let's use this fact to prove the proposition: (Z/nZ)^× looks like exactly the set of a-bar in Z/nZ such that — any guesses? Yes, exactly: gcd(a, n) = 1; or to use a more abbreviated terminology, a and n are relatively prime.

98
00:42:28,080 --> 00:43:05,848
既然我们已经建立了关于 Z 子群的这些基本算术事实，这证明起来就很容易了。不过这里有一个微妙之处：单是写下这个集合，有什么微妙的地方？是的，首先是我们不知道……写下这个集合有一个小问题：
So this is very easy to prove now that we've established these basic arithmetic facts about the subgroups of Z. Yes, well, the first is that we don't know... Well, yeah, is there another issue with writing this down?

99
00:43:05,848 --> 00:43:24,347
大家会注意到我左边取的是 Z/nZ 中的 a-bar，而在右边条件里我把那横线去掉了！这并没有唯一的提升：若有一个等价类 a-bar，它有许许多多可能的代表元。
Because you'll notice that I'm taking a-bar in Z/nZ, and on the right part I'm removing the bar! Now there's no unique lift: if I have an equivalence class a-bar, there are many possible representatives.

100
00:43:24,347 --> 00:43:53,839
所以这里有一个微妙点：如果一个等价类的某个代表元与 n 互素，那么该等价类中的每一个元素都与 n 互素。这不难证明，但在写出这个定义时确实是一个需要注意的细节。不过无论如何，我接下来证明这个命题。
So there's a subtle point: namely, if one element of an equivalence class is relatively prime to n, then every element of that equivalence class is also relatively prime to n. Now this isn't difficult to show, but it's certainly a subtlety in writing down this thing. But anyway, I'll go on and prove this proposition.

101
00:43:55,359 --> 00:44:27,029
首先证明右边包含于左边：如果 gcd(a, n) = 1，那么由引理，我能写出 aZ + nZ = Z；或者换言之，1 落在其中——这等价，也是直接推出来的结论：我可以把 1 写成 ar + ns，
First, the right-hand side is included in the left-hand side: if gcd(a, n) = 1, then by the lemma, I can write aZ + nZ = Z; or put another way, 1 is in here — it's equivalent, but it's also just something following from 1 in aZ + nZ:

102
00:44:27,029 --> 00:45:10,160
对某些整数 r 和 s。这为什么重要呢？这告诉我们 ar − 1 ∈ nZ，换句话说，a-bar · r-bar = 1-bar。所以 a-bar 显然属于(Z/nZ)^×。
I can write 1 = ar + ns for some r and s. Why is this significant? Well, this tells us that ar - 1 is in nZ, or put another way, a-bar * r-bar = 1-bar. So a-bar is certainly in Z/nZ cross ((Z/nZ)^×).

103
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:50,055
反方向的证明非常类似：左边包含于右边。做完全同样的事：如果 a-bar · c-bar = 1-bar，那么我可以写出 ac − 1 = nb，对某个整数 b。于是 1 = ac − nb 属于 aZ +nZ。
Now the converse is very similar: left-hand side contained in right-hand side. We do the same sort of thing: if I have a-bar * c-bar = 1-bar, then I can write ac - 1 = nb for some integer b. So 1, which is ac - nb, is in aZ + nZ. But this thing is just gcd(a, n)Z,

104
00:45:50,055 --> 00:46:20,200
但这正是 gcd(a, n)Z；如果 1 属于形如 dZ 的子群，那么 d 必须是 ±1；它必须是 1，因为如果 d 是 2, 3, 4 之类，1 不可能落在里面——2 在 Z 中没有乘法逆元。这就证明了结论！给出了单位群的结构。
and if 1 is in this subgroup of the form dZ, then d must be plus or minus 1; it must be 1, because if this were 2 or 3 or 4, 1 couldn't possibly be in there — 2 does not have a multiplicative inverse in Z, for instance. So this proves the result! This gives the structure.

105
00:46:20,200 --> 00:46:48,612
例：(Z/pZ)^×长什么样？请讲。除 0 以外的所有元素。除了 0 之外的一切：1, 2, 3 到 p − 1 显然都与 p 互素。所以这就是 {1, 2, ... ..., p − 1}。
Example: what does (Z/pZ)^× look like? Yeah, go ahead. Everything except 0. Everything except for 0: 1, 2, 3, up to p - 1 are certainly relatively prime to p. So this is {1, 2, ... ..., p - 1}.

106
00:46:48,612 --> 00:47:30,399
另一个例子：更一般地，(Z/p^eZ)^× 的阶是多少？有人知道吗？是的：p^e − p^{e−1}。为什么？他给出的答案是 p^e − p^{e−1}。
Another example: More generally, what is the order of (Z/p^eZ)^×? Any takers? Yes: p^e - p^{e-1}. Why? So the answer he gave is p^e - p^{e-1}.

107
00:47:30,399 --> 00:47:56,893
他给出的答案参考了数论背景，但其实有一种非常简单的组合方式来看出这一点。请讲。完全正确：就是除掉 p 的倍数之外的所有元素。p 的倍数有哪些？1·p, 2·p, 3·p,
Basically, the answer he gave makes reference to some number-theoretic background, but there's a very simple combinatorial way of seeing why that's true. Yes, go ahead. Exactly: this is everything except the multiples of p. What are the multiples of p?

108
00:47:56,893 --> 00:48:35,884
直到 p^{e−1}·p。这些恰恰是与 p 不互素的数。所以我们从总共 p^e 个元素开始，扣掉不互素的 p^{e−1} 个元素，剩下的就是与 p 互素的 p^e − p^{e−1} 个元素。
1*p, 2*p, 3*p, up to p^{e-1}*p. These are exactly the things not relatively prime to p. So we started out with the set of p^e things, we took away p^{e-1} things which were not relatively prime, and we're left over with p^e - p^{e-1} things which are relatively prime.

109
00:48:35,884 --> 00:48:50,430
好，这是对模算术的初步介绍。下一节课我们要做的，是进一步发挥第一部分的主题，即这种加法结构。
Okay, so this is a first introduction to modular arithmetic. But what we're going to be doing in the next class is riffing further on the theme from the first part, which is this additive structure.

110
00:48:50,430 --> 00:49:00,643
乘法结构要到课程后半部分才会详细讨论，但现在了解它肯定很有用，因为你需要它作为工具。
This multiplicative structure is something we'll get to only later in the course in greater detail, but it's certainly useful to have seen now because you need it as a tool.

111
00:49:00,643 --> 00:49:32,599
而我们接下来要讨论的，正如我之前提到的：如果有群 G 的某个正规子群 H，我们将能考察陪集空间，记作 G/H（即 H 在 G 中的陪集集合），
But what we're going to start discussing later on is that, as I alluded to before, if I have some normal subgroup H of G, we're going to be able to look at the coset space, which we're going to denote G/H (the set of cosets of H in G),

112
00:49:32,599 --> 00:50:10,953
并且会看到存在一个自然的同态——存在一个从 G 到 G/H 的自然同态，它具有满射的像，且核恰好是 H。
and we're going to see that there's a natural homomorphism — there is a natural homomorphism from G to G/H with full image and kernel exactly H.

113
00:50:10,953 --> 00:50:29,633
这将回答大家上次课提出的一个问题：给定一个正规子群，是否总存在以该正规子群为核的同态？今天我们对整数群 Z 这一特例给出了肯定回答。
And this will answer a question that one of you had in the last class: if one has a normal subgroup, is there always a homomorphism which has that normal subgroup as its kernel? We've answered that in the special case of the group Z today.

114
00:50:29,633 --> 00:50:49,017
为什么？因为 Z 的正规子群就是 Z 的所有子群，即形如 nZ 的子群；而且我们看到存在一个从 Z 到 Z/nZ 的自然映射，我称之为"red"—
Why? Well, because the normal subgroups of Z are exactly the subgroups of Z, which are of the form nZ; and we saw that there's a natural map, which I called "red" — short for reduction,

115
00:50:49,017 --> 00:51:03,944
—即约化映射（reduction）的简称，这是常用的术语——我没有完整检验，但验证这个映射是同态非常简单。
because this is the terminology used — from Z to Z/nZ, and I didn't verify this, but it's a very easy verification that this map is a homomorphism.

116
00:51:03,944 --> 00:51:20,531
其实某种意义上我已经验证了：也就是如果我写 a-bar + c-bar，它就等同于 (a + c)-bar，
Well, I did do it in some sense: that was just the idea that if I write a-bar + c-bar, that was the same thing as (a + c)-bar, because this map just takes a to a-bar.

117
00:51:20,531 --> 00:51:42,003
因为该映射就是把 a 映为 a-bar。所以这验证了它是一个同态；此外按构造它是满射；最后——这是一个很好的检验——它的核恰好是 nZ。
So this is a verification of the fact that this is a homomorphism; moreover it's surjective by construction; and finally — this is a good verification — the kernel is exactly nZ.

118
00:51:42,003 --> 00:52:00,305
下一节课我们要做的，就是看为什么这对群的任意正规子群都成立。很好，谢谢大家！
So what we're going to do next time is see why this continues to work for any normal subgroup of a group. Great, thanks!
